---
// Why Codeyard next to Claude Code or Codex, for a beginner, in three themes
// with one illustrative example each, and a short FAQ (#374, plan of #372,
// section 2.3). The page names no script, setting, file or JSON: the technical
// detail is in the docs, linked from each theme and each answer. The old ids of
// the eight features stay as aliases on the line that took over their content
// (section 3.3 of the plan), and the FAQ questions keep the ids they have on
// /faq/, so that its redirect can lead to them.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import { href } from "../lib/links";

const themes = [
  { id: "agree", title: "Agree before you build" },
  { id: "independent", title: "Let agents work, independently" },
  { id: "verify", title: "Verify before you accept" },
];
const title = Object.fromEntries(themes.map((theme) => [theme.id, theme.title]));

const questions = [
  { id: "replace", title: "Why not just use Claude Code or Codex?" },
  { id: "who", title: "Do I need to know programming?" },
  { id: "cost", title: "What does it cost?" },
  { id: "data", title: "Where does it run, and what data reaches AI providers?" },
  { id: "existing", title: "Can I use an existing project?" },
  { id: "too-heavy", title: "When is this way of working worth it?" },
];
const question = Object.fromEntries(questions.map((item) => [item.id, item.title]));

// The old anchors of a feature and its technical layer, kept where its content now stands.
const old = (name: string) => [name, `${name}-h`, `${name}-t`, `${name}-words`];
---
<Base title="Features" description="Why Agent Codeyard next to Claude Code or Codex: agree on what to build before it is built, let agents work on features independently, and verify every change before you accept it. With a short FAQ.">
  <PageHead title="Features" lede="Claude Code and Codex write the code. Codeyard adds the way of working around them: agree on what to build, let the agents work on their own, and check every change before you accept it.">
    <p>Codeyard writes no code itself and is not a hosted service. It runs on your machine or server, and the coding tools you already use do the writing.</p>
    <ol class="contents">
      {themes.map((theme) => <li><a href={`#${theme.id}`}>{theme.title}</a></li>)}
      <li><a href="#faq">Questions</a></li>
    </ol>
  </PageHead>

  <Section id="agree" title={title.agree}>
    <Fragment slot="note">In the docs: <a href={href("docs/detailed-workflow/#clarify-the-requirements-project-grill")}>Clarify the requirements</a>, <a href={href("docs/detailed-workflow/#approve-the-plan")}>Approve the plan</a>.</Fragment>
    <p id="planning"><span id="planning-h"></span>A description of what to build always leaves questions open, and a coding tool usually answers them with a guess that ends up in the code. Codeyard settles them first.</p>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li>{old("requirements").map((id) => <span id={id}></span>)}<b>Project Grill asks what your description leaves open:</b> the goals, the users, the scope and the limits, and only what would change the product.</li>
      <li><b>Requirements are agreed before building.</b> They count once you approve them, and every feature is checked against them. When a feature reaches a decision only you can make, it stops and asks instead of guessing.</li>
      <li>{[...old("architecture"), "planning-t", "planning-words"].map((id) => <span id={id}></span>)}<b>You review and approve the plan.</b> A second AI reviews the architecture and the order of the features, and nothing is built until you approve them. A later change to the plan needs your approval again.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>Illustrative example.</b> A gym wants an application where trainers record attendance for their classes and members see their own training history. Before anything is built, you are asked whether members may see each other's training history. You answer: only their own history. The plan then builds users and roles first, then classes and attendance, then the member's history view.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="independent" title={title.independent}>
    <Fragment slot="note">In the docs: <a href={href("docs/detailed-workflow/#features-side-by-side")}>Features side by side</a>, <a href={href("docs/detailed-workflow/#run-a-feature-unattended")}>Run a feature unattended</a>.</Fragment>
    <p id="control"><span id="control-h"></span>Once the plan is approved, agents can build features while you do something else.</p>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li><b>Several features in parallel.</b> Features that are independent enough, because they do not change the same part of the code, can be built at the same time.</li>
      <li>{old("isolation").map((id) => <span id={id}></span>)}<b>Each in its own working copy and branch.</b> Every feature gets its own Git worktree, so a half-finished change of one cannot end up in another, and your main copy stays untouched.</li>
      <li><b>Progress outlasts a session.</b> A short handoff note says what is done and what remains, so a session that stops is continued by the next one, not started over.</li>
      <li>{old("unattended").map((id) => <span id={id}></span>)}<b>Unattended when you want.</b> One command takes a feature from its Issue to a proposed change without asking you anything, within fixed limits on sessions, repairs and review rounds. When it needs your decision, it stops with the question.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Codeyard is not a scheduler that runs every feature of the plan in one batch: you start each run, and separate runs can work side by side.</p>
    <p><b>Illustrative example.</b> Once users and roles exist, you start attendance in the evening, and beside it an unrelated feature, the gym's opening hours. In the morning, one is a proposed change with its checks and review; the other waits for one question of yours.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="verify" title={title.verify}>
    <Fragment slot="note">In the docs: <a href={href("docs/verification-and-completion/")}>Verification and completion</a>, <a href={href("docs/risk-policy/")}>Risk policy</a>.</Fragment>
    <p id="quality"><span id="quality-h"></span>A coding tool that says it is done gives its own judgment. Codeyard asks for evidence before a change reaches you.</p>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li>{old("evidence").map((id) => <span id={id}></span>)}<b>Tests and project checks.</b> Your project's tests and checks run on exactly the version you are offered, and for each agreed requirement the change says what shows it is met.</li>
      <li>{[...old("review"), ...old("risk")].map((id) => <span id={id}></span>)}<b>Independent review by a different model,</b> where the risk policy asks for it: an AI of another provider, which cannot edit the change, reads it. As shipped, every change is reviewed; a project can let low-risk changes, such as a typo in the docs, skip the review, and their merge then asks for your approval.</li>
      <li><b>Findings are triaged, fixed and reviewed again.</b> Every finding gets a written decision: fix it now, move it to a new Issue, or accept it with a reason. Critical and major findings are always fixed, unless you make an exception yourself, and the fix is reviewed again.</li>
      <li>{old("authority").map((id) => <span id={id}></span>)}<b>The evidence, and your decision.</b> The proposed change carries the checks, the review and the decisions on the findings. No script merges: you do. A change to the project's own rules, design decisions or workflow is marked as needing your explicit approval.</li>
      <li>{old("learn").map((id) => <span id={id}></span>)}<b>Learning from the runs.</b> Every run leaves a record on your machine, and an analysis you start proposes improvements to your rules and checks: <a href={href("docs/technical-reference/#learning-from-your-runs")}>Learning from your runs</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A check proves what it examines, no more: neither the checks nor the review promise that every problem is found.</p>
    <p><b>Illustrative example.</b> The first version let every signed-in user open the history view. The reviewer reported it; the fix added the check “a member who requests another member's history is refused”, and the change was reviewed again before it reached you.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="faq" title="Questions">
    <Fragment slot="note">The practical questions, from installing to stopping a run, are answered in the <a href={href("docs/")}>docs</a>.</Fragment>
    <div class="faq">
      <details id="replace">
        <summary id="replace-h">{question.replace}</summary>
        <p>Claude Code and Codex can plan, ask questions, run tests and review on their own. Codeyard turns that into one route that every change follows: agreed requirements, an approved plan, fixed roles, checks and a review, and progress the next session continues. It writes no code itself, and you keep your tools and your editor.</p>
        <p class="needs">The route in detail: <a href={href("docs/detailed-workflow/")}>How the workflow works</a>.</p>
      </details>
      <details id="who">
        <summary id="who-h">{question.who}</summary>
        <p>To understand what it does, no. To use it, you work in a terminal with Git and GitHub; it is not a no-code tool. You also need to judge the questions the AI asks and the changes it proposes.</p>
        <p class="needs">The basic ideas first: <a href={href("docs/new-to-agentic-coding/")}>New to agentic coding?</a></p>
      </details>
      <details id="cost">
        <summary id="cost-h">{question.cost}</summary>
        <p>Codeyard itself is free and open source, under the MIT licence. You pay for the AI models through your own Claude Code and Codex accounts, by subscription or paid API use, with their usage limits. The amount depends on the size of a change, the number of review rounds and the models you choose, so no price per feature can be given.</p>
        <p class="needs">The models per role: <a href={href("docs/agent-roles/")}>Agent roles and models</a>; what a few recorded runs took: <a href={href("docs/engineering-records/#tradeoff")}>More rounds find more, and cost more</a>.</p>
      </details>
      <details id="data">
        <summary id="data-h">{question.data}</summary>
        <p>Codeyard runs on your own machine or server; no Codeyard service sits in between. The coding tools send what they read and write, such as your code and the Issue, to their providers, Anthropic for Claude Code and OpenAI for Codex, under those providers' terms. The run records stay on your machine; your requirements, Issues and proposed changes are in your repository on GitHub.</p>
        <p class="needs">The detail: <a href={href("docs/configuration-and-security/#data")}>Which data goes where</a>.</p>
      </details>
      <details id="existing">
        <summary id="existing-h">{question.existing}</summary>
        <p>Yes. Codeyard first reads your repository and reports what it would add, without changing anything. You review the addition like any other change, and checks that already fail are recorded as known, so they stay visible.</p>
        <p class="needs">All steps: <a href={href("docs/adopting/")}>Adopting Agent Codeyard in an existing repository</a>.</p>
      </details>
      <details id="too-heavy">
        <summary id="too-heavy-h">{question["too-heavy"]}</summary>
        <p>Questions, a plan, checks and a review cost time and model use for every change. That pays off for software you will extend and maintain, where a wrong assumption costs more later. For a quick experiment, a coding tool on its own is faster, and a project can ask for less process on low-risk changes.</p>
        <p class="needs">How much checking each kind of change gets: <a href={href("docs/risk-policy/")}>Risk policy</a>.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>

<script>
  // A link to a question, or to an id inside a closed answer, opens it.
  const open = () => {
    const target = location.hash && document.getElementById(decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1)));
    const details = target?.closest("details");
    if (details && !details.open) {
      details.open = true;
      target.scrollIntoView();
    }
  };
  open();
  addEventListener("hashchange", open);
</script>
